WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.633 --> 00:00:17.676
The next three weeks at Green Gables were busy as Anne got ready to go to Queen's College.

2
00:00:18.376 --> 00:00:20.337
Anne had many lovely clothes to take.

3
00:00:21.037 --> 00:00:24.938
It was Matthew who bought Anne's clothes, and Marilla no longer objected.

4
00:00:25.558 --> 00:00:30.840
Anne put on her new green silk dress, and recited a poem for Matthew and Marilla in the

5
00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:36.282
kitchen. Marilla watched Anne's bright, animated face and graceful movements.

6
00:00:36.943 --> 00:00:38.543
A tear came to Marilla's eye.

7
00:00:38.563 --> 00:00:41.185
"My poem made you cry, Marilla!"

8
00:00:41.545 --> 00:00:42.285
Anne exclaimed.

9
00:00:42.305 --> 00:00:47.848
"No, I wasn't crying over that," Marilla sniffed.

10
00:00:47.868 --> 00:00:51.370
"I was thinking of the little girl you used to be, Anne.

11
00:00:52.051 --> 00:00:54.012
I wish you had stayed a little girl.

12
00:00:54.612 --> 00:00:57.714
You've grown up now, and you're going away to college.

13
00:00:58.354 --> 00:01:04.057
You look so tall and stylish and so so different in that dress.

14
00:01:04.878 --> 00:01:06.840
I just get lonely thinking about it."

15
00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:12.664
"Marilla!" Anne took Marilla's hands and looked tenderly into Marilla's eyes.

16
00:01:12.684 --> 00:01:15.386
"I haven't changed, not really.

17
00:01:15.406 --> 00:01:19.529
The real me—back here—is just the same.

18
00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:25.834
I will always be your little Anne, who will love you and Matthew and dear Green Gables every

19
00:01:25.874 --> 00:01:26.835
day of her life."

20
00:01:27.595 --> 00:01:32.860
Matthew listened, and with suspicious moisture in his eyes, he got up and went outside.

21
00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:39.385
"Well now, I guess she hasn't been spoiled," Matthew said to himself proudly.

22
00:01:39.405 --> 00:01:43.028
"I guess interfering sometimes never did any harm either.

23
00:01:43.609 --> 00:01:45.790
She's smart, pretty, and loving too.

24
00:01:46.411 --> 00:01:47.492
She's been a blessing to us.

25
00:01:48.052 --> 00:01:49.594
It was a lucky mistake that Mrs.

26
00:01:49.654 --> 00:01:50.314
Spencer made.

27
00:01:50.835 --> 00:01:52.576
But I don't believe it was luck.

28
00:01:53.217 --> 00:01:56.860
No, I believe Anne was sent as a gift from heaven because we needed her."

29
00:01:57.552 --> 00:01:59.673
The day came for Anne to go to college.

30
00:02:00.313 --> 00:02:04.394
After many tearful good-byes, Anne's life as a college student began.

31
00:02:05.795 --> 00:02:09.636
On the first day at Queen's, students met each other and their professors.

32
00:02:10.396 --> 00:02:13.377
Afterwards Anne left the college and went to her boarding house.

33
00:02:14.117 --> 00:02:18.558
She looked dismally at her little room, with its picture-less walls and empty bookcase.

34
00:02:19.279 --> 00:02:22.139
A tear came into her eye as she thought of Green Gables.

35
00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:24.000
Anne started to cry.

36
00:02:24.648 --> 00:02:28.749
And a flood of tears would have come if Josie Pye had not appeared at Anne's door.

37
00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:35.191
In the joy of seeing a familiar face, Anne forgot that she and Josie were not good friends,

38
00:02:35.371 --> 00:02:36.111
to say the least.

39
00:02:36.131 --> 00:02:39.412
"I'm glad you came," Anne said sincerely.

40
00:02:39.432 --> 00:02:42.113
"You've been crying," Josie remarked.

41
00:02:42.133 --> 00:02:43.813
"I suppose you're homesick.

42
00:02:44.434 --> 00:02:46.014
Some people have no self-control.

43
00:02:46.674 --> 00:02:50.695
You really shouldn't cry because your nose and eyes get red, and then you seem red all over."

44
00:02:51.276 --> 00:02:52.476
With that, Josie left.

45
00:02:53.064 --> 00:02:54.485
Anne scowled and cried again.

46
00:02:55.606 --> 00:02:57.627
Soon Anne's homesickness subsided.

47
00:02:58.248 --> 00:03:00.929
She threw herself into her studies and made new friends.

48
00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:07.214
She decided to try for the academic awards: the gold medal for the best student in general and

49
00:03:07.254 --> 00:03:10.656
a scholarship to Redmond University for the best student in English.

50
00:03:10.676 --> 00:03:14.819
"I'll win that scholarship if hard work can do it," she thought.

51
00:03:14.839 --> 00:03:17.881
"Wouldn't Matthew be proud if I got a BA?"

52
00:03:19.062 --> 00:03:22.524
After the Christmas holidays, the students settled down to hard work.

53
00:03:23.208 --> 00:03:30.371
By this time the medal contestants were narrowed down to three: Gilbert Blythe, Anne Shirley, and Lewis Wilson.

54
00:03:31.011 --> 00:03:35.393
The scholarship was more doubtful, with any one of six students being a possible winner.

55
00:03:36.074 --> 00:03:41.656
Anne's rivalry with Gilbert was as intense as ever, but for some reason the bitterness had gone out

56
00:03:41.696 --> 00:03:41.936
of it.

57
00:03:43.077 --> 00:03:46.518
Spring came, but at Queen's there was talk only of exams.

58
00:03:47.279 --> 00:03:50.260
"Ruby, sometimes I feel like exams mean everything.

59
00:03:50.872 --> 00:03:56.354
But when I look at the flowers blossoming, the exams don't seem as important," said Anne.

60
00:03:57.034 --> 00:03:58.814
Ruby Gillis did not share this view.

61
00:03:59.314 --> 00:04:03.836
To her, exams were very important, far more important than flowers!

62
00:04:04.476 --> 00:04:09.597
Besides, Anne was sure to pass all her exams while Ruby was not so certain.

63
00:04:10.637 --> 00:04:14.998
It was the morning the exam results were to be posted on the notice board at Queen's College.

64
00:04:15.759 --> 00:04:18.179
Anne and Jane Andrews walked down the street together.

65
00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:23.225
Jane was smiling and happy; exams were over and she had passed at least.

66
00:04:23.965 --> 00:04:25.266
Anne was pale and quiet.

67
00:04:25.926 --> 00:04:30.028
In ten minutes she would know who had won the medal and the scholarship.

68
00:04:30.048 --> 00:04:35.250
"I don't have the courage to march up to that notice board, Jane," Anne said.

69
00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:37.431
"You must read it and tell me.

70
00:04:38.071 --> 00:04:39.992
And try to do it as quickly as possible.

71
00:04:40.592 --> 00:04:45.534
If I failed, just tell me, and whatever you do, don't try to sympathize with me.

72
00:04:46.175 --> 00:04:47.375
Promise me, Jane."

73
00:04:48.055 --> 00:04:52.533
Jane promised solemnly, but as it happened, there was no need for such a promise.

74
00:04:53.294 --> 00:04:58.976
Anne and Jane found a group of boys carrying Gilbert Blythe on their shoulders and yelling, "Hooray for

75
00:04:59.016 --> 00:05:00.997
Gilbert, medalist!"

76
00:05:01.877 --> 00:05:04.378
Anne felt a pang of defeat and disappointment.

77
00:05:05.139 --> 00:05:06.279
So Gilbert had won.

78
00:05:07.019 --> 00:05:09.000
Well, Matthew would be sorry.

79
00:05:09.581 --> 00:05:11.241
He had been so sure she would win.

80
00:05:11.802 --> 00:05:17.304
And then somebody called out, "Three cheers for Anne Shirley, winner of the scholarship!"

81
00:05:18.052 --> 00:05:23.674
Suddenly many students were around them, and Anne was the center of a laughing, congratulating group.

82
00:05:25.134 --> 00:05:30.576
She was pushed and pulled and hugged, but she managed to whisper to Jane, "Oh, won't Matthew and

83
00:05:30.616 --> 00:05:31.756
Marilla be pleased?

84
00:05:32.396 --> 00:05:34.237
I must write home right away."

85
00:05:34.957 --> 00:05:40.118
After the scholarship and medal ceremony, Anne went home to Avonlea with Matthew and Marilla.

86
00:05:40.678 --> 00:05:42.779
Diana was at Green Gables to greet her.

87
00:05:42.799 --> 00:05:48.245
"It's so good to be home again," cried Anne, "and it's good to see you.

88
00:05:48.265 --> 00:05:51.028
I have so many things to tell you.

89
00:05:51.528 --> 00:05:55.091
But now I'm tired—tired of being studious.

90
00:05:55.732 --> 00:06:00.916
I'll spend two hours tomorrow lying on the grass, thinking of absolutely nothing."

91
00:06:00.936 --> 00:06:04.259
"You've done so well, Anne," said Diana.

92
00:06:04.279 --> 00:06:08.302
"I suppose you won't be teaching, now that you've won the scholarship?"

93
00:06:09.023 --> 00:06:11.065
"No, I won't," said Anne.

94
00:06:11.085 --> 00:06:14.588
"I'll go to the Redmond University in September."

95
00:06:15.296 --> 00:06:20.259
"Jane's going to teach in Newbridge," said Diana, "and Gilbert is going to teach too.

96
00:06:20.819 --> 00:06:21.299
He must.

97
00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:26.722
His father can't afford to send him to college next year, so he will work instead and save

98
00:06:26.762 --> 00:06:27.643
up the money for it."

99
00:06:28.363 --> 00:06:29.644
Anne felt a little dismayed.

100
00:06:30.284 --> 00:06:33.526
She had expected Gilbert to go to Redmond University as well.

101
00:06:34.127 --> 00:06:36.688
What would she do without their rivalry to inspire her?

102
00:06:37.408 --> 00:06:40.070
And what would she do without her friends from Avonlea?
